Bug: a Latin-1 character swallows the characters after it

latin1.c has a comment "/* på*/" with the å in Latin-1 (0xE5). The server reads
0xE5 as the lead byte of a three-byte UTF-8 sequence and skips the next two
bytes, which are the "*/". The comment runs to the end of the file, the only
trace is a "comment through eof" warning, and -push finds no symbol. The same
file in UTF-8 passes with this expected.

Cause: convertUtf8() (src/encoding.c) collapses each multibyte character to one
space, so that byte offsets equal the character offsets the editor counts. It
decides the length from the lead byte alone and never checks that the following
bytes are continuation bytes (10xxxxxx). Latin-1 ä/å (0xE4/0xE5) eat two
characters, Ä/Å/Ö (0xC4/0xC5/0xD6) one. A swallowed "*/", '"' or newline wrecks
the rest of the file. The Emacs client writes preloads in the buffer's own
coding system, so an open Latin-1 buffer breaks the same way.

Likely fix: collapse a sequence only when its continuation bytes are there, and
otherwise take the byte as one character. That is exactly right for Latin-1 and
other single-byte encodings, which are one byte per character, leaves UTF-8 as
it is, and needs no -encoding option (removed in 0ef4eace). Only Latin-1 text
that happens to form valid UTF-8 is misread, which is rare. A unit test in
encoding_tests.c can pin the byte level.

Decide first: whether to also report invalid UTF-8, once per file, so a file in
an unexpected encoding is no longer silent.

Found on the Mac, session 36694da1.
